03545cam a2200445 a a6860509SIRSI070108s2007    ctuaf    b    001 0 eng    a  2007000612  a9780313340932 (hbk. : alk. paper)  a0313340935 (hbk. : alk. paper)  a9780325071350 (pbk. : alk. paper)  a0325071357 (pbk. : alk. paper)  a(Sirsi) cis5760103  a(OCoLC-M)79256700  a(OCoLC-I)276014912  aDLCcDLCdBAKERdBTCTAdC#PdYDXCPdIXAdDLC  an-us---00aPS153.N5bB526 20071 aBishop, Rudine Sims.0(SIRSI)74817810aFree within ourselves :bthe development of African American children's literature /cRudine Sims Bishop.  aWestport, Conn. :bGreenwood Press,c2007.  axvi, 295 p., [8] p. of plates :bill. ;c27 cm.  aIncludes bibliographical references (p. [275]-282) and index.0 aBefore 1900 : sowing the seeds of African American children's literature -- For the children of the sun : African American children's literature begins to bloom -- Breaking new ground : Arna Bontemps and some of his contemporaries -- "Give them back their own souls" : change and the need for change -- African American poetry for children : soft Black songs -- African American picture books take shape : authenticating the worlds of Black children -- African American picture books expand : celebrating the past, reflecting the present -- African American illustrators of children's books : nine pacesetters -- Newer African American illustrators : expanding possibilities, maintaining traditions -- African American children's fiction : illuminating the life of the people -- African American contemporary realistic fiction : focus on teens and preteens -- African American historical fiction : telling a people's story.  a1 0aAmerican literaturexAfrican American authorsxHistory and criticism.0(SIRSI)1989872 0aChildren's literature, AmericanxHistory and criticism.0(SIRSI)2096165 0aAfrican Americans in literature.0(SIRSI)990562 0aAfrican American children in literature.0(SIRSI)1747667 0aAfrican American childrenxBooks and reading.0(SIRSI)2492416 0aAfrican AmericansxIntellectual life.0(SIRSI)1091883  c0hGREEN-H  aMONORECUnit/23 October 2007/pha  fWATTbdruid:tq045gb8801ctq045gb8801_00_0001.jp2dRalph Wardlaw Watt Memorial Book Fund  bWritten by a leading authority, this book overviews the history of African American children's literature from the antebellum South to the present day. The volume places African American children's literature in its social, political, and cultural contexts, discusses its origins in songs and folklore, and examines its importance as a vehicle for promoting respect for the African American heritage. Included are discussions of major authors and illustrators as well as important genres of children's literature. Librarians of children's literature will treasure this book as a convenient overview of a fascinating subject, while students will value it as a guide to the African American literary achievement and the treatment of social issues in fiction.1Nielsenx9780313340932x20160528  a6860509  a(Sirsi) cis5760103  a(Sirsi) cis5760103ffi18e6a963-07a8-5cac-83e1-152679f363dcse2a5f49d-e6d6-5391-a943-7dd5e45c89b204338cam a2200709Ii a12375375SIRSI20260722205230.9140116t20142014nyu    d      000 1 eng d  a  2001092470  a9781442490017q(hbk.)  a1442490012q(hbk.)  z9781442490000q(pbk.)  z1442490004q(pbk.)  a(Sirsi) a12375375  aMLYbengerdacMLYdMLYdOCLCOdNYPdOCLCAdBDXdABGdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLdOCLCOdOCLCAdCNEDMdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLdOCLCOdOCLdVHPdALDdAL5CWdOCLCQdOCLCOdLKCdOCLCAdCStdUtOrBLW  an-cn-pi  aSTFA 4aPR9199.3.M6bA65 2014  aJF MONTGO L1 aMontgomery, L. M.q(Lucy Maud),d1874-19420http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8101834692ca9814f-cd91-4eeb-91cf-a6d9b119fe0810aAnne of Green Gables /cL.M. Montgomery.  aAladdin hardcover edition. 1aNew York :bAladdin,c2014. 4c©2014  a440 pages ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aAnne, an eleven-year-old orphan, is sent by mistake to live with a lonely, middle-aged brother and sister on a Prince Edward Island farm and proceeds to make an indelible impression on everyone around her.10aShirley, Annec(Fictitious character)vJuvenile fiction.0(SIRSI)338016911aShirley, Annec(Fictitious character)vFiction.17aShirley, Annec(Fictitious character)2fast0(OCoLC)fst011166830http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1116683 0aOrphansvJuvenile fiction.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh20081087260(SIRSI)2103666 0aCountry lifezPrince Edward IslandvJuvenile fiction.0(SIRSI)1008751 0aPrince Edward IslandxHistoryy20th centuryvJuvenile.0(SIRSI)96237 0aCanadaxHistoryy1867-1914vJuvenile.0(SIRSI)1000726 1aOrphansvFiction. 1aCountry lifezPrince Edward IslandvFiction. 1aPrince Edward IslandxHistoryy20th centuryvFiction. 1aCanadaxHistoryy1867-1914vFiction. 7aCountry life.2fast0(OCoLC)fst008814050http://id.worldcat.org/fast/881405 7aOrphans.2fast0(OCoLC)fst010484330http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1048433 7aCanada.2fast0(OCoLC)fst012043100http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1204310 7aPrince Edward Island.2fast0(OCoLC)fst012048040http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1204804 7a1867-19992fast 4aHistory. 4aFiction. 4aJuvenile works. 7aHistory.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014116280http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1411628 7aFiction.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014237870http://id.worldcat.org/fast/14237870(SIRSI)3322979 7aJuvenile works.2fast0(OCoLC)fst014116370http://id.worldcat.org/fast/1411637  aC0bSTF  aCATDCUnit/date20180316/bz/CATCLASSUnit  aCatalogedby/CATCLASSUnit/dcw1/date20180319  aIDPortalTest/date20180319/catalogedby dcw1/Symphony  a11  a(OCoLC-M)868229892  bWhen Anne Shirley arrives at Green Gables, she surprises everyone: first of all, she's a girl, even though Marilla Cuthbert and her brother Matthew specifically asked for an orphan boy to help around the farm. And second of all, she's not just any girl: she has bright red hair, a wild imagination, and can talk a mile a minute. But she also has a sweet disposition and quick wit, and Anne (with an "e" of course-it's so much more distinguished!) soon finds her place in Avonlea, making a friend in her neighbor Diana Barry and attending the local school, where she spurns the advances of the popular and handsome Gilbert Blythe when he commits the ultimate sin of making fun of her hair. Anne has a temper as fiery as her hair and a knack for finding trouble, and she also has a big heart and a positive attitude that affects everyone she meets. This classic and beloved story makes a wonderful gift and keepsake.1Nielsenx9781442490017x20180326  a12375375  a(Sirsi) ocn868229892ffi3ff41699-a872-56ce-92af-b17951ab7788s2043e1d7-1b7d-51da-8e17-4a5e611329e703889cgm a2200601Ia a9733035SIRSI20260722204604.3vd cvaizq010516p20102000cau090 g          vleng d  a078063344X  a97807806334451 a085391895121  a(Sirsi) a9733035  aNNUbengcN15dOCLCQdRCJdUtOrBLW1 aengafrejengjfrehfre  aRCJL 4aPN1997.2b.B47 201004a791.43/7222200aBest in showh[videorecording] /c[presented by] Warner Bros. Pictures ; Castle Rock Entertainment.  aBurbank, CA :bWarner Home Video,cc2010.  a1 videodisc (90 min.) :bsd., col. ;c4 3/4 in.  aDVD, NTSC; region 1; widescreen presentation; Dolby Digital 5.1.  aEnglish and French language soundtracks with optional subtitles in English and French; closed-captioned in English for the hearing impaired.1 aJennifer Coolidge, Christopher Guest, John Michael Higgins, Michael Hitchcock, Eugene Levy, Jane Lynch, Michael McKean, Catherine O'Hara, Parker Posey, Fred Willard.  aDirected by Christopher Guest ; written by Christopher Guest & Eugene Levy ; produced by Karen Murphy ; editing, Rob Leighton ; director of photography, Roberto Schaefer.  aOriginally released as a motion picture in 2000.  aA comic look at dog show participants (and the pooches who love them).  aSpecial features: Cast & crew information; a